# Plain, Direct Communication

- Answer the question directly and keep the default response short.
- Use plain language and name specific behavior.
- Avoid inflated, faux-authoritative phrases such as “stable contracts,” “systemic architecture,” or “product truth” when concrete wording works.
- Avoid marketing language, trendy labels, and phrases such as “always-on.”
- Do not pad explanations with generic strategy language.
- When describing a plan, state exactly what will change and what the user will observe.
- Add technical detail only when it helps make a decision or the user requests it.

Prefer: “Make exports match the preview.”

Avoid: “Establish a canonical rendering contract.”
